{"root": {"type": "root", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Delta is the fourth letter of the Greek alphabet, and it may be the single most useful symbol in science. The uppercase Δ means \"change in\" and appears in every physics formula involving a difference, from Δt to ΔH. The lowercase δ handles the small and the precise: infinitesimal changes, the Dirac delta function, the Kronecker delta, partial charges in chemistry, and chemical shifts in NMR. Beyond math, delta names the triangular landforms at river mouths, a key risk measure in options trading, and a configuration of three-phase electrical wiring.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Common Delta Expressions", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"tag": "ul", "type": "list", "start": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"type": "listitem", "value": 1, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Δx = x₂ − x₁", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — change in x", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 2, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Δy/Δx", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — slope, rate of change", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 3, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "ΔG = ΔH − TΔS", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — Gibbs free energy", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 4, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Δ = b² − 4ac", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — discriminant", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 5, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "δᵢⱼ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — Kronecker delta", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 6, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "δ(x)", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — Dirac delta", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 7, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "δ+ / δ−", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — partial charges", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "listitem", "value": 8, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "∇²f", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " — Laplacian", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}], "listType": "bullet", "direction": null}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Delta Symbol FAQ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What does the delta symbol mean?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Uppercase Δ means change or difference (Δx, ΔH, Δv), and also the discriminant b² − 4ac and the Laplace operator. Lowercase δ means a small change, the tolerance in epsilon-delta limits, the Dirac or Kronecker delta, or a partial charge in chemistry. For the purely mathematical meanings, see ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"id": "6330de5c22b073c92ad8904a", "type": "link", "fields": {"url": "https://www.useoctree.com/blog/delta-math-understanding-change-difference", "newTab": false, "linkType": "custom"}, "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 3, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "what delta means in mathematics", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ".", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "What is the delta sign?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "The delta sign usually means the uppercase Greek delta, Δ, used as shorthand for \"change in\". ΔT is a change in temperature, Δp a change in momentum and ΔG a change in Gibbs free energy. In chemistry a Δ written over a reaction arrow means the reaction is heated.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Is ∆ (U+2206) the same as Δ (U+0394)?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "No. Δ (U+0394) is the Greek capital letter delta; ∆ (U+2206) is the INCREMENT math symbol. They look nearly the same, but software treats them as different characters, so a search for \"ΔT\" will not find \"∆T\". Use Δ in documents and equations; the Mac shortcut Option + J types ∆.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "How do you type the delta symbol?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "On Windows, hold Alt and type 235 on the numeric keypad for δ; for Δ type 0394 and press Alt + X in Word. On a Mac, switch to the Greek input source and press Shift + D for Δ or D for δ (Option + J gives the increment sign ∆). In HTML write &Delta; or &delta;, or copy Δ δ from the box above.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h3", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "How do you write delta in LaTeX?", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Use ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "$\\Delta$", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " for uppercase and ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "$\\delta$", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " for lowercase, in math mode: ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "$\\Delta x = x_2 - x_1$", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ". For an italic capital delta use ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "$\\varDelta$", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " with amsmath, and do not confuse ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\delta", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " with ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\partial", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " (∂) or ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\nabla", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": " (∇).", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"tag": "h2", "type": "heading", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Writing Documents with Greek Symbols", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Lab reports, physics derivations, and chemistry papers lean on delta constantly, and keeping Δ, δ, ∂, and ∇ straight is a mark of careful typesetting.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Octree is an AI-powered LaTeX editor that knows the difference between ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\Delta", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ", ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\delta", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ", and ", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": "\\partial", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 16, "version": 1}, {"mode": "normal", "text": ", so you can write naturally and get a correct, compiled document every time.", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 0, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}, {"type": "paragraph", "format": "", "indent": 0, "version": 1, "children": [{"mode": "normal", "text": "Create professional documents with Greek symbols at https://useoctree.com", "type": "text", "style": "", "detail": 0, "format": 1, "version": 1}], "direction": null, "textStyle": "", "textFormat": 0}], "direction": null}}
